# @WORKFLOW_NAME@

This is a native MAF YAML workflow with an ordinary asynchronous Python binding.
It fingerprints explicit local files, joins bounded reads in input order, and
emits the actual binding receipt. It invokes no agent or external service.

Create a project-local environment and install requirements.lock with hash
verification. The lock was generated for the tested CPython 3.12/Linux profile;
regenerate and test a lock for another target platform/Python version. The
requirements.txt file records direct requirements, not the transitive lock.
MAF 1.4.0/Power Fx also requires a supported .NET runtime. The example was tested
with .NET 8; set DOTNET_ROOT to its installation path when it is project-local.
Do not assume installing a Python wheel also provisions the native runtime.

```sh
python run.py --project /path/to/project --input inputs.json --jobs 4
```

Adapt inputs.json to existing project files. Paths are project-relative; escaping
the project and duplicate canonical files are rejected. Keep input files stable
or use a read-only snapshot: parallel hashing is not a filesystem snapshot.

Fresh-run success requires the workflow's emitted output to equal the actual
successful binding receipt. Exceptions and tool failures cannot become an empty
success. This example does not implement checkpoints/resume or external effects.
Add those semantics explicitly for a real workflow. Native MAF execution must
be tested separately from direct binding tests and YAML inspection.

Replace the example's stages/bindings with the actual requested procedure before
claiming the requested workflow is complete. Keep conditions and deterministic
checks in code; register an agent only for residual judgment within scope.
